-- Create card, "Planning Statement: Policy" and "Planning Statement: Assessment":
-- the key policies are now quoted VERBATIM (key wording only), in italics, with long
-- quotations on their own line. Appended to the existing guidance (it states that it
-- overrides the earlier lines about quoting), so any edits already made to these rows
-- in the admin console are kept. Safe to run twice: skipped once the section is there.

UPDATE admin_console.create_doc_types
SET structure_notes = COALESCE(structure_notes, '') || E'\n\n' || $q$Quoting key policies verbatim (firm; this overrides anything above about when to quote)
For each KEY policy only, quote its key wording word for word from the ticked policy sources. A key policy is one that has a relevance note (the ones described, not the ones listed by reference and title only). Do not quote the policies that are only listed by reference and title. Quote the operative requirement, test or criterion that this application turns on, not the whole policy and not every part of it. Quote the same way for key national policy (for example the NPPF paragraphs that matter) where its text is supplied.
- Choosing what to quote. Decide which words matter from what you have been given about THIS proposal: the policy's relevance note, any Drafting Issue notes, ticked meeting transcripts, the proposal facts in the other ticked sources, and the document so far. Quote the part of the policy that those show the application engages (for example the criterion the council or the scheme turns on), not simply its opening sentence. If you have little to go on about the proposal, quote the policy's operative test and say in your reply that you chose it without much context.
- Length of quotations. Keep every quotation short: only the operative words, normally one sentence or part of one, about 15 to 40 words and never more than about 60. One quotation per policy; a second only if it is a separate test the application turns on. Do not quote introductory or explanatory text, do not quote a whole list of criteria (quote only the criterion that applies), and use [...] to cut out anything in the middle that is not needed. If a long passage seems necessary, quote the decisive part and summarise the rest in your own words.
- Verbatim, no exceptions. Copy the words exactly as they appear in the source: no paraphrase, no tidying, no changed or reordered words. To leave words out, use [...] at that point. Never reconstruct a quotation from memory, from the relevance note, or from general knowledge. Use only wording that is in the ticked sources or that the user has given in the chat.
- If the wording of a key policy is not in the sources, do not quote it. Give the short description from its relevance note and add [POLICY WORDING REQUIRED: policy reference].
- Italics. Every quotation is in italics and inside quotation marks, wrapped in <em>.
- Layout. A quotation that fits within about one line stays in the sentence. Anything longer than one line (about 25 words) starts on its own line as a block: <blockquote><p><em>"the quoted text"</em></p></blockquote>, introduced by the line before it (for example "Policy P5 states:").
- Shape of a key policy entry: the policy reference and title, one sentence on why it is relevant (from the relevance note), then the quotation. Keep the ▪ bullet for the reference and title line and put a longer quotation in a block directly beneath it.$q$
WHERE slug = 'planning_statement_policy'
  AND COALESCE(structure_notes, '') NOT LIKE '%Quoting key policies verbatim%';

UPDATE admin_console.create_doc_types
SET structure_notes = COALESCE(structure_notes, '') || E'\n\n' || $q$Quoting key policies verbatim (firm; this overrides anything above about when to quote)
Within each issue, quote the key wording of the one to three policies that decide that issue, word for word from the ticked sources (the Linked Policies and any policy text supplied). Quote the operative test or requirement the issue turns on, not the whole policy and not every linked policy. The other policies that bear on the issue are named by reference and title only, grouped, with no quotation.
- Choosing what to quote. For each issue, work out which policy words matter from what you have: the issue itself, its notes (the intended argument), any transcript, the proposal facts in the other ticked sources and the document so far. Quote the part of the policy that the issue actually turns on (the criterion the proposal has to meet, or the one in dispute), not simply the policy's opening sentence. If you have little about the proposal for an issue, quote the policy's operative test and say in your reply that you chose it without much context.
- Length of quotations. Keep every quotation short: only the operative words, normally one sentence or part of one, about 15 to 40 words and never more than about 60. One quotation per policy for an issue; a second only if it is a separate test the issue turns on. Do not quote introductory or explanatory text, do not quote a whole list of criteria (quote only the criterion that applies), and use [...] to cut out anything in the middle that is not needed. If a long passage seems necessary, quote the decisive part and summarise the rest in your own words.
- Verbatim, no exceptions. Copy the words exactly as they appear in the source: no paraphrase, no tidying, no changed or reordered words. To leave words out, use [...] at that point. Never reconstruct a quotation from memory, from a note, or from general knowledge. Use only wording that is in the ticked sources or that the user has given in the chat.
- If the wording of a policy that needs quoting is not in the sources, do not quote it. Describe it briefly from what is supplied and add [POLICY WORDING REQUIRED: policy reference].
- Italics. Every quotation is in italics and inside quotation marks, wrapped in <em>.
- Layout. A quotation that fits within about one line stays in the sentence. Anything longer than one line (about 25 words) starts on its own line as a block: <blockquote><p><em>"the quoted text"</em></p></blockquote>, introduced by the sentence before it (for example "Policy P11 requires that:").
- Placement. Name the policy, give the quotation, then assess the proposal against it in your own words. Do not repeat the quotation in the assessment, and do not let quotations replace the assessment: the quotation states the test, the paragraphs after it do the work.$q$
WHERE slug = 'planning_statement_assessment'
  AND COALESCE(structure_notes, '') NOT LIKE '%Quoting key policies verbatim%';
